            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's core identification of the wall as 'Salvation' and its counterfeit as self-built false security is directly established by the cited scriptures. Isaiah 60:18 explicitly provides the definition ('thou shalt call thy walls Salvation'), and Zechariah 2:5 illustrates the divine nature of this enclosure ('a wall of fire'). The negative pole is equally well-supported by Proverbs 18:11 (wealth as a high wall in conceit) and Ezekiel 13:10-15 (the whitewashed wall of false peace). Even the common interpretations acknowledge salvation and protection as the primary meaning, making the book's specific, unified definition compelling and deeply rooted in the text.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The wall symbolizes salvation (God's protective covenant enclosure), while its counterfeit is self-built false security.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"The counterfeit is self-built.\",\n    \"Wealth acts as a high wall 'in his own conceit.'\",\n    \"The whitewashed wall represents false peace that is flattened by a storm.\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter conflates the 'hedge' of the vineyard (phragmos) with the 'middle wall of partition' in Ephesians 2:14, arguing that the wall of salvation was opened inward rather than abolished entirely.\",\n    \"The chapter identifies the tower in the vineyard as the temple (a house for the Name) by linking it to the Tower of Babel and Proverbs 18:10.\",\n    \"The chapter interprets the winepress as producing either wine (from good grapes) or the blood of men (from wild grapes in judgment).\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"The wall is a versatile architectural metaphor whose meaning shifts significantly based on context (e.g., protection, a literal obstacle as in Jericho, or a barrier of hostility as in Ephesians 2), rather than acting as a single, fixed typological symbol of salvation and its counterfeit.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"A general metaphor for protection, separation, obstacles, or boundaries.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"SAME_CORE_BROADER\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter denies that all biblical walls map to a unified symbolic binary of divine salvation versus self-built false security.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Ephesians 2:14 focuses on a 'dividing wall of hostility' (representing the Law separating Jews and Gentiles) which Christ destroys, not a wall of salvation.\",\n    \"The walls of Jericho in Joshua 6 and Hebrews 11 are literal obstacles overcome by faith, not symbols of self-built false security.\",\n    \"Because ancient cities uniformly relied on walls, the imagery naturally produces polyvalent, localized metaphors rather than a strict overarching cipher.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Rests on a direct scriptural definition (Isaiah 60:18, 'thou shalt call thy walls Salvation').\",\n    \"Unifies the positive uses (Isaiah, Zechariah) and the negative uses (Proverbs, Ezekiel) into a coherent symbolic binary (divine enclosure vs. self-built counterfeit).\",\n    \"Explains the specific language of false peace and whitewashed mortar in Ezekiel 13 as the exact counterfeit to God's genuine salvation.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Easily accommodates narrative events (Jericho) and distinct theological arguments (Ephesians 2) without needing to harmonize them into the 'salvation' framework.\",\n    \"Acknowledges the flexible, context-dependent nature of poetry and wisdom literature.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Isaiah 60:18\",\n    \"Zechariah 2:5\",\n    \"Proverbs 18:11\",\n    \"Ezekiel 13:10-15\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
